          </p>

          <p>
            The important line is the certification. The listing states USDA Organic, NSF Certified for Sport and Non-GMO Project Verified, and says the powder is tested for banned substances. NSF Certified for Sport is the program drug-tested athletes rely on, because it screens batches for prohibited substances and checks that the label matches the contents. Very few organic plant proteins carry it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buyer feedback is solid but not glowing. The vanilla listing sat at about 4.2 out of 5 stars across nearly 9,000 ratings, with texture the most common complaint and some buyers reporting a recent price rise and a change in taste. As with most plant proteins, it is thicker and earthier than whey. Mixing it with plant milk or blending it into a smoothie is the usual fix.
