          </h2>

          <p>
            Raw Organic Protein is the tub many people mean when they search for Garden of Life raw protein. Its listing states 22 g of protein, 4 g of BCAAs and 5 g of fiber in 130 calories per scoop, with 0 g of sugar and about 1 g of net carbohydrate. A tub holds 20 servings, and the vanilla listing carried about 4.2 out of 5 stars across more than 13,000 ratings at the time of writing.
          </p>

          <p>
            The protein comes from a long sprouted blend: organic pea protein, sprouted brown rice protein and a list of sprouted grains, seeds and legumes including amaranth, buckwheat, millet, quinoa, chia, lentil, garbanzo, flax, pumpkin and sesame, plus chlorella. Pea and rice do most of the work. The long list of sprouts is a marketing strength more than a nutritional one, since each contributes a small amount.
          </p>

          <p>
            Raw refers to Garden of Life's claim that the powder is made at low temperatures. There is no good evidence that this improves the quality of the protein your body absorbs, since cooking and digestion break proteins down anyway. It is a reasonable preference if you like minimally processed foods, but not a reason to choose Raw Organic over Sport if your goal is muscle.
          </p>

          <p>
            The flavor system matters more. The vanilla ingredient list includes organic erythritol and organic stevia leaf extract, with guar gum for texture. Erythritol is a sugar alcohol that most people tolerate in small amounts but that can cause bloating in sensitive people. The tub also adds digestive enzymes, including lipase, lactase and papain, and the Bacillus subtilis DE111 probiotic.
          </p>

          <p>
            Raw Organic Protein's certifications are USDA Organic, Non-GMO Project Verified, vegan, kosher, NSF Certified Gluten-Free and NSF Contents Certified, per the listing. That is a respectable set, but it does not include NSF Certified for Sport, and the difference is explained in the certifications section below. If you compete in a tested sport, Raw Organic is not the right tub.
          </p>
