            At 150 calories, Raw Organic Meal is light for a meal. Garden of Life positions it as an all-in-one shake with fiber and superfoods, and the high fiber helps with fullness. But most people would need to add fruit, nut butter or oats to make it a real meal. If you want a more substantial plant meal shake, our <a href="/huel-protein-powder-review/">Huel protein powder review</a> covers a 400-calorie option with 40 g of protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            Raw Organic Protein and Greens adds organic vegetables and greens, including spinach, kale, broccoli, alfalfa grass, carrots and beets, to a 20 g protein base, with probiotics and enzymes, per the listing. The comparison chart puts it at about 110 calories and 1 g of fiber per serving. It is sweetened with organic stevia. It suits people who want one morning shake that covers protein and some greens.
          </p>

          <p>
            Neither is the best choice if protein is the priority. A 20 g plant serving is fine between meals but short of ideal after training, and both tubs cost more per 25 g of protein than they first appear because of the smaller dose. They make sense if you specifically want the fiber or the greens. Otherwise, Sport or a plain pea isolate gives you more protein for the money.
          </p>

