        <div id="certifications" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Organic, NSF and the Badge That Matters
          </h2>

          <p>
            Garden of Life tubs carry a lot of badges, and they do not mean the same thing. USDA Organic means the ingredients are grown and processed to the USDA's organic standard. Non-GMO Project Verified covers genetically modified ingredients. Neither tests for banned substances or heavy metals. They are farming and sourcing standards, which are worthwhile but answer a different question from safety testing.
          </p>

          <p>
            NSF runs several programs. NSF Certified for Sport, which the Sport tub carries per its listing, screens batches for a long list of banned substances and checks that the label matches the contents. NSF Certified Gluten-Free, which appears on the Raw Organic tubs, only checks gluten. NSF Contents Certified checks that the product contains what the label says. Only the first is the sport certification athletes need.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Look for the words Certified for Sport.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">An NSF logo on a Garden of Life tub does not by itself mean sport testing. Only the Sport tub claims NSF Certified for Sport. Check the exact product and flavor on NSF's database before relying on it.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Heavy metals are the other concern with plant proteins, because legumes, rice and greens can take up lead and cadmium from soil. Organic farming does not prevent that. A sport certification includes contaminant checks, which is another reason the Sport tub is the safer pick within the range. Our <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">is protein powder safe</a> guide explains how to read testing claims across brands.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are pregnant, buying for a teenager or simply want the most cautious option, choose a product with a sport certification you can look up, and check with a doctor or registered dietitian before adding a supplement. Our <a href="/best-organic-protein-powder/">best organic protein powder</a> guide compares organic tubs that also carry independent testing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="digestion" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Sweeteners, Probiotics and Digestion
          </h2>

          <p>
            The Raw Organic tubs list organic erythritol and organic stevia in their vanilla flavors, and Protein and Greens lists organic stevia. The Sport listing says it has no artificial sweeteners, and buyer reviews attribute its sweetness to stevia; check the ingredient panel on your flavor. If stevia's aftertaste bothers you, Garden of Life is a hard brand to love, since most of its flavored tubs rely on it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Erythritol is the ingredient to watch if you have a sensitive stomach. It is a sugar alcohol that is better tolerated than most, but in larger amounts it can cause bloating or a laxative effect. Some buyers on the Sport listing also report stomach upset they attribute to stevia. If you have had trouble with protein shakes before, try a half serving for the first week.
          </p>

          <p>
            Every tub in the range includes probiotics, usually Bacillus subtilis DE111, and the Raw Organic tubs add digestive enzymes. Some people find enzyme blends help them tolerate plant protein, but the evidence for adding them to a shake is limited. Treat them as a bonus rather than a reason to buy, and do not rely on a protein powder as your probiotic if you have a digestive condition.
          </p>

          <p>
            Texture is the most common complaint across the range, as it is for plant proteins in general. Buyers frequently describe Sport as thick and slightly gritty in water, and Raw Organic as smoother but thinner. Blending with ice and plant milk helps both. If you want ideas, our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-smoothies/">best protein powder for smoothies</a> guide covers how to mask plant protein textures.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Who Should Buy It, and the Alternatives
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy Garden of Life Sport if you want organic plant protein with a full 30 g dose and a sport certification you can check. It suits vegans, people with dairy allergies, plant-based athletes who are drug tested, and cautious buyers who want contaminant screening along with organic sourcing. It is the strongest product in the range and usually the best value per gram too.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy Raw Organic Protein if you want a lighter, organic protein for smoothies and do not need sport testing, and you tolerate erythritol. Buy Raw Organic Meal if you want a high-fiber, low-calorie meal-style shake. Skip the Raw Organic line if you train hard, because the 20-22 g serving is short of ideal, or if you compete, because it lacks the sport certification.
          </p>

          <p>
            The closest alternative is Vega Protein + Recovery, formerly Vega Sport, with 30 g of protein and NSF Certified for Sport status at a similar cost per 25 g. It uses pea and seed proteins rather than organic legumes, so choose it if you prefer its flavors or texture. Our <a href="/vega-protein-powder-review/">Vega protein powder review</a> covers that range, and our <a href="/orgain-protein-powder-review/">Orgain protein powder review</a> covers Garden of Life's sister brand.
          </p>

          <p>
            The budget alternative is Naked Pea, a single-ingredient pea isolate with no sweeteners that costs far less per gram. It suits people who blend their shakes and do not need certification. Whatever you choose, anyone who is pregnant, managing kidney disease or taking medication should check with a doctor or registered dietitian first; this review is not medical advice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming any NSF logo means sport tested</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Garden of Life's Raw Organic tubs carry NSF Certified Gluten-Free and NSF Contents Certified badges, not NSF Certified for Sport. Only the Sport tub claims the sport program. If you are drug tested, check the exact product and flavor in NSF's Certified for Sport database before you buy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Raw Organic for post-workout shakes</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Raw Organic Protein gives 22 g of plant protein per scoop, and the Meal and Greens tubs give 20 g. After hard training, a plant serving closer to 30 g is a better target to cover leucine. Sport delivers that in one serving, often at a lower cost per 25 g.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring erythritol in the Raw tubs</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The Raw Organic vanilla flavors list organic erythritol along with stevia. Most people tolerate it, but some get bloating or loose stools from sugar alcohols. If you have a sensitive stomach, start with a half serving, or choose Protein and Greens or Sport, which do not list erythritol.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating organic as a safety guarantee</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                USDA Organic covers how ingredients are grown, not heavy metals or banned substances. Plant proteins can take up lead and cadmium from soil whatever the farming method. For contaminant screening, look for a sport certification, which within this range means the Sport tub.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using Raw Organic Meal as a full meal</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                At 150 calories per scoop, Raw Organic Meal is closer to a snack than a meal for most adults. Drinking it alone at lunch can leave you hungry and reaching for food later. Add fruit, oats or nut butter, or choose a higher-calorie meal shake if you need a real replacement.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

