          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Garden of Life protein powder good?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The Sport tub is one of the best plant proteins available, with 30 g of organic protein per serving and NSF Certified for Sport status, per its listing. The Raw Organic line is a reasonable lighter protein, with 20-22 g per serving and erythritol in the vanilla flavors, but no sport certification. For most buyers, Sport is the Garden of Life tub to choose.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which Garden of Life protein is NSF Certified for Sport?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Of the plant proteins we checked, only Garden of Life Sport Organic Plant-Based Protein claims NSF Certified for Sport on its listing. The Raw Organic tubs carry NSF Certified Gluten-Free and NSF Contents Certified, which are different programs that do not screen for banned substances. Always confirm the exact product and flavor on NSF's database.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much protein is in Garden of Life Raw Organic Protein?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The Raw Organic Protein listing states 22 g of protein, 4 g of BCAAs and 5 g of fiber in 130 calories per scoop, with 0 g of sugar and about 1 g of net carbohydrate. Raw Organic Meal and Raw Organic Protein and Greens list 20 g each. Figures can vary slightly by flavor, so check your tub's label.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What does raw mean on Garden of Life protein?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Garden of Life uses raw to describe protein it says is made at low temperatures, with sprouted ingredients and added enzymes. There is no good evidence that low-temperature processing improves the protein your body absorbs, since digestion breaks proteins down anyway. It is a reasonable preference for minimally processed food, not a performance advantage.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does Garden of Life protein contain stevia?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most flavored Garden of Life proteins do. The Raw Organic Protein and Raw Organic Meal vanilla flavors list organic erythritol and organic stevia, and Protein and Greens lists organic stevia. The Sport listing says it has no artificial sweeteners, and buyers attribute its sweetness to stevia. Check the ingredient panel on your flavor if you avoid it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Who owns Garden of Life?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Garden of Life is owned by Nestlé Health Science, which also holds a majority stake in Orgain. That makes the two brands sister companies. The ownership has no effect on the labels or certifications described in this review, but it is useful context if you prefer buying from independent supplement brands.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Garden of Life Sport or Vega better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They are close. Both list 30 g of plant protein per serving with NSF Certified for Sport status and land at a similar cost per 25 g at typical prices. Garden of Life Sport is USDA Organic; Vega Protein + Recovery is not. Choose Garden of Life if organic matters to you, and Vega if you prefer its flavors or texture.
              </p>
            </div>

